b  when the child is exposed to useful  forms of language over and  over and over again the child learns  these forms instinctively  for children learning comes through  massive repetition of language  in context so to learn verbs like  children do  you need to do the same or something  similar don't focus on conjugation  tables which are selected curated forms  of language taken out of context  focus on using and reusing resources  that represent the language as  it is naturally used and you will absorb  the proper  verb forms over time personally my  favorite beginner resources for  absorbing natural language  are spoken dialogues with an  accompanying transcript  these dialogues naturally will be full  of verbs that are conjugated  appropriately  according to context so if you repeat  and review them  often enough you will soon learn what  form of the verb  matches that exact context over time  this exposure will help your brain  implicitly understand the patterns that  verbs in your target language follow  and you'll never have to mentally  consult a conjugation table  ever again here's a quick tip you can  use to really help your brain focus on  learning verbs  every time you see a verb in a text one  of your dialogues for example  use a highlighter to mark the word on  your page so that it stands out you can  even use different colors to categorize  verbs by tense for example which is  really helpful  when you're starting out in a language  as you gain more and more exposure to  natural language you will come across an  ever increasing number of verbs and word  forms you might be tempted to learn them  all to know them all so that you always  know just the right verb form for  any situation but do you really need to  know  all of the verbs to speak a language  well and what does it mean to  know a verb anyway does it mean that you  know all these forms  doesn't mean that you know how to  conjugate the verbs  in all its tenses aspects and moods  thankfully the 